{# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Operator report: one-glance freshness across every LLM/agent surface.

   Not an alert — a report, run on demand, that answers "which surface is
   stale and by how much" in a single table. The silence detectors above tell
   you when ONE index crosses ITS threshold; this tells you the state of all
   of them at once, which is what you want in your hand while diagnosing.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- #}
[llm_surface_freshness]
description = Most-recent-event age per LLM/agent observability surface. age_hours = NEVER means the index has never received a single event, which is a different failure from "went quiet" and must not be reported as a missing row.
{# NOTE: plain {# #}, never {#- -#}. The whitespace-stripping form eats the
   newline on BOTH sides of this comment, which welds `description = ...` and
   `search = ...` into a single line. Splunk then reads the whole search as
   part of the description, leaves `search` undefined, and the report runs to
   HTTP 200 with zero rows — it does not error. That shipped and reached the
   live instance; see the deployed-value assertion in
   tests/templates/test_savedsearches_render.py.

   The makeresults/mvexpand/join shape is load-bearing and is NOT equivalent to
   a bare `tstats latest(_time) by index`. tstats returns NO ROW AT ALL for an
   index that has never received an event, so the surface most in need of
   attention is precisely the one that silently vanishes from the output. The
   left join against a synthesized roster keeps every surface on the report and
   renders the never-populated ones as NEVER. The since-removed index=otel was
   in exactly this state — a missing row that read as "nothing to see here",
   and it stayed invisible until this report was built. #}
search = | makeresults | eval surface="{{ splunk_docker_llm_freshness_indexes | join(',') }}" \
  | makemv delim="," surface | mvexpand surface \
  | join type=left surface [| tstats latest(_time) as last_seen WHERE index IN ({{ splunk_docker_llm_freshness_indexes | join(',') }}) by index | rename index as surface] \
  | eval age_hours=if(isnull(last_seen), "NEVER", round((now() - last_seen) / 3600, 1)) \
  | eval last_event=if(isnull(last_seen), "-", strftime(last_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")) \
  | table surface age_hours last_event | sort surface
dispatch.earliest_time = -30d
dispatch.latest_time = now
enableSched = 0
is_visible = 1
disabled = 0
